  "Question": "A rectangular garden \\(50\\) feet long and \\(10\\) feet wide is enclosed by a fence. To make the garden larger, while using the same fence, its shape is changed to a square. By how many square feet does this enlarge the garden?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(100\\)",
    "B": "\\(200\\)",
    "C": "\\(300\\)",
    "D": "\\(400\\)",
    "E": "\\(500\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Using the same fence keeps the perimeter unchanged. The rectangle's perimeter is\n\\[\n2(50+10)=120\\text{ feet}.\n\\]\nA square has four equal sides, so each new side is \\(120\\div4=30\\) feet. The original area is \\(50\\cdot10=500\\) square feet, and the new area is \\(30\\cdot30=900\\) square feet. Thus the garden grows by\n\\[\n900-500=\\boxed{400\\text{ square feet}},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
